WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.569 --> 00:00:09.200
Simon Stevens&gt;&gt; EPRR?

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.720
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; No, no.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.350
I think we carry on?

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.749
Thank you, right, okay.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:20.960
Let me turn to item 10, and we have delegation
of primary medical services functions.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:21.960
Ian?

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.939
Ian Dodge&gt;&gt; The purpose of this item is to
seek formal agreement from the Board to the

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:32.051
delegation of the function of primary medical
services commissioning to 51 CCGs as a kind

00:00:32.051 --> 00:00:33.330
of second wave.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:43.649
This is in the context of extant NHS England
policy to support applications from CCGs to

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:50.380
take on these functions as part of the joining
up of NHS commissioning functions to provide

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:57.480
a better focus on place and better integration
of services.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.200
Secondly, within the context of the robust
management of conflicts of interest, including

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.500
our new strength and guidance, which we have
just discussed.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:12.130
Thirdly, and I have touched on this already,
a formal assurance process, overseen by commissioning

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:16.420
operations directorate and our regions.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:24.750
Then fourthly, what I would describe as an
initial, broadly positive set of information

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:35.970
of the likely benefits from co-commissioning,
including through a survey of CCGs that have

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:43.090
gone for either delegated or joint arrangements,
discussions with NHS England, regional teams

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:49.290
who have been involved in their functions
hitherto and many continue to, and some emerging

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.439
examples of CCGs.

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:55.950
However, it is still relatively early days
to be able to make a full assessment.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:04.500
We are nonetheless keen to get independents
and clear feedback on the efficacy of the

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.880
new arrangements, including through a formal
independent evaluation which is being undertaken

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.620
by the policy research unit for commissioning.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.680
We will have, as I say, further data in due
course.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:25.410
On the basis of that brief explanation, I
invite the Board to approve the delegations.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:29.173
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; This is an extension of what
we are already doing.

00:02:29.173 --> 00:02:32.200
We have some understanding of how that is
working in practice, and we know we will have

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.519
more in due course.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:38.380
On that basis, may I invite the Board to approve
the proposals in the paper?

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:39.760
Good, thank you very much.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.850
I would like to move now onto item 11, which
is the emergency preparedness, resilience

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:50.000
and response paper, and to invite Bruce to
speak to it.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.900
Bruce Keogh&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Chairman.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:59.450
Under the Civil Contingencies Act 2004 and
the Health and Social Care Act, this Board

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:04.970
has responsibility for taking over the effective
functioning of the NHS in the face of significant

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:12.760
disruption, and the aim of this paper is to
assure the Board that we are assuming that

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:20.880
responsibility effectively but not to induce
any sense of complacency.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.979
I will not run through the paper itself, but
I would like to make a few remarks about some

00:03:24.979 --> 00:03:27.169
additional issues.

00:03:27.169 --> 00:03:35.950
The first is that about a month ago we were
part of an exercise called Unified Response,

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:42.160
which was one of the biggest exercises ever
undertaken in Europe to simulate a major disaster,

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.390
with over 1,000 casualties; there were teams
from three other countries.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:53.361
It had been over 18 months in the planning,
and the end result of that was the health

00:03:53.361 --> 00:03:59.609
component of that stood out very well with
no major deficiencies exposed.

00:03:59.609 --> 00:04:07.040
The second issue that I would like to raise,
which is in the particular context of clinical

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.579
experience, is that we have had two learning
events to try and understand what happened

00:04:12.579 --> 00:04:17.040
in the Paris attacks and what could have been
done better.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.479
The most recent of those was 8th March in
Birmingham, and major trauma centres, the

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:25.010
military and other people of interest took
part.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:31.720
We are currently awaiting a call with the
French Health Ministry to exchange our learning

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:39.780
with them, but I think it would be fair to
say that we have a good understanding of that

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:46.170
and that we believe our major hospital incident
plan is equivalent to their white plan and

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:51.840
it is as effective, and that our mass casualty
plan is the equivalent of the French red plan

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.820
and is just as effective.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:05:02.580
The third thing I would like to say is that
during the course of the year we had to deal

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:10.140
with the Ebola epidemic emerging in West Africa,
and I think it would be remiss of me not to

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.600
say that our first lesson that we learn from
that, and I would like to say this on behalf

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:20.510
of the Chief Nursing Officer and myself as
Medical Director, was an example of remarkable

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:26.161
selfless professionalism from many people
working in the NHS who demonstrably put their

00:05:26.161 --> 00:05:30.840
own health at risk for the sake of others.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.420
I think we owe them a debt of gratitude.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:41.550
I believe that we have acquitted ourselves
pretty well in the Ebola epidemic.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:47.210
The risk that ensues from that is that somehow
or other some complacency does emerge, and

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:54.130
that all our protocols, our structures that
we put in place and the learnings, just somehow

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:57.280
or other get stuck on a shelf and they gather
dust.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:04.480
To ensure that that does not happen, and that
we maximise the relationships that were built

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:08.620
throughout the United Kingdom – not just
England – for attacking Ebola, and that

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:15.370
they are cast into the DNA of the way we do
business with highly infectious communicable

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:20.970
diseases, we have asked Mike Jacobs, who was
the lead clinician for the Ebola epidemic,

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:29.139
who works at the Royal Free Hospital, to help
us distil those learnings and to embed them.

00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:36.030
The fourth area that I would like to pass
comment on is that pandemic flu remains on

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:42.060
the National Risk Register as a very significant
and potential problem, and that the Department

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:47.110
of Heath will be conducting a major exercise
to try and understand how we would deal with

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:51.580
that in October this year.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:58.890
The fifth and sixth issues are related, and
they refer to the junior doctors’ strike.

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:08.710
So next week, between 08.00 on Wednesday and
08.00 on Friday, junior doctors will be undertaking

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:13.120
another of their strike actions.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.889
Emergency services will be covered.

00:07:15.889 --> 00:07:21.090
We have undertaken, or are in the process
of undertaking, a data collection to understand

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:25.210
how each trust will deal with this and to
assure ourselves that we are in a reasonable

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:26.210
place.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:31.830
That data collection closes at 17.00 this
evening, but early indications are that we

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:39.870
do not expect there to be any difference with
this 48 hour disruption to the previous 48

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:40.870
hour.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:47.690
However, there is one small caveat to that,
and that is that the strike next week will

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:53.540
coincide with the changeover of about a fifth
of junior doctors in the country between different

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.290
organisations.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:08:00.210
So we have had to work closely with Health
Education England to ensure that we have the

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:05.100
right induction processes in place to ensure
that those junior doctors who are changing

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:11.139
institutions, and particularly those who might
be on call the following weekend, are properly

00:08:11.139 --> 00:08:13.780
inducted.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:18.820
The consequence of that is that most trusts
will be running two inductions processes,

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:24.710
one on Wednesday and one on Friday, to ensure
that that is covered.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:34.560
The next issue is the subsequent strike in
the last week of April, which I think will

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.250
elevate the potential disruption to the NHS
both in terms of safety and maintenance of

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:45.490
service in a way that we have not seen so
far, because during working hours junior doctors

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:49.260
will be withdrawing cover for emergency services.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:57.370
Now, we propose to undertake data collection
and initiate our assurance process immediately

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:02.370
after next week’s strike in order that we
can have a deeper understanding of exactly

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:10.670
what the implications are for that, so that
we will have that analysis complete a week

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:11.790
in advance of the strike.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:16.450
We would then propose to share that with the
British Medical Association in order to put

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:22.610
in place the agreed protocols that we have
with them and the mitigations, to ensure that

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:27.450
the risk to both the NHS and our patients
is minimised.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:28.860
Thank you.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:33.050
May I invite comments and queries?

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:41.830
No, well I think, Bruce, thank you for that
very thorough report and we will watch each

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:45.680
of those developments with great concern.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.440
However, can we also endorse what we said
about the Ebola response, which I thought

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:56.750
exemplified some of the very best practices
of medics and other clinicians and other staff

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:58.140
right across the NHS.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:02.240
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Right, we turn now to committee
reports.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:03.240
We have, first of all, commissioning committee.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.240
David.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.030
David Roberts&gt;&gt; Nothing to add to the report,
Malcolm.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.360
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.360
Investment committee, Moira?

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.360
Moira Gibb&gt;&gt; Nothing to add to the report.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:16.470
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; David again, audit and risk?

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:18.899
David Roberts&gt;&gt; Nothing to add or report on.

00:10:18.899 --> 00:10:23.150
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Right, that brings me to any
other business, on which I have two items

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:25.100
if nobody else does.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:33.050
First is just to record that the Board yesterday
made some awards to celebrate outstanding

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:39.350
performance by several of our staff who had
been identified by their line managers.

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:44.550
They were people who had gone the extra mile
and I was very happy to host, along with Simon

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:50.480
and the other members of the Board, a ceremony
and also a luncheon to celebrate what our

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.870
key staff do for us.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:57.210
The second is to record again, as I did at
the outset of the meeting, that this is Margaret’s

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:03.860
last meeting as a Board member of NHS England,
and to invite the Board in a moment to express

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:07.040
their appreciation for all that she has done.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:12.820
Margaret joined us in 2012 as one of the pioneers
on what was called the NHS Commissioning Board.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:19.740
She was then company secretary and legal counsel
to John Lewis partnership, and brought many

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:26.080
of those values of retail and wrapping the
package, as it were, around the client, around

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:31.360
the patient that has helped to steer us over
the past four years in our thinking about

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:36.960
the way in which we should organise our activities
through NHS England; and also, Margaret, consistently

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.670
around issues around governance and propriety
of behaviour, for which we are immensely grateful

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:42.670
to you.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:47.910
So may I, on behalf of all the Board, wish
you well now in your new role as Chair of

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:53.220
Action Aid, and thank you on behalf of all
of us for the enormous contribution you have

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:58.770
made to our thinking and to our proceedings
over the past four years.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:11:59.770
Thank you very much.

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:07.540
So I now invite the Board to pass the usual
resolutions, we move into private business,

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:08.880
and I declare this meeting closed.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.140
Thank you very much.

